(Chemical Research Note: Aspirin Hydrolysis Study) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
T*
() Tj
T*
(This document describes a small laboratory investigation of acetylsalicylic acid,) Tj
T*
(commonly known as aspirin. The research objective was to observe hydrolysis under) Tj
T*
(mild alkaline conditions and identify the main products formed during the reaction.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Aspirin has the molecular formula C9H8O4 and a molar mass of 180.16 g/mol. In the) Tj
T*
(presence of sodium hydroxide and water, aspirin can hydrolyze to form salicylic acid) Tj
T*
(and acetate. The reaction was monitored at room temperature using pH measurements) Tj
T*
(and thin-layer chromatography.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Experimental setup: 0.50 g of aspirin was dissolved in 25 mL of ethanol-water) Tj
T*
(solution. A dilute NaOH solution was added dropwise while stirring. Samples were) Tj
T*
(collected after 5, 15, 30, and 60 minutes. The expected compounds were aspirin,) Tj
T*
(salicylic acid, sodium acetate, ethanol, and water.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Observed result: the aspirin spot decreased over time, while a new spot consistent) Tj
T*
(with salicylic acid appeared after 15 minutes. The solution became slightly basic) Tj
T*
(after addition of NaOH. These observations support the interpretation that aspirin) Tj
T*
(underwent base-assisted hydrolysis.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example facts for extraction:) Tj
T*
(- Aspirin has molecular formula C9H8O4.) Tj
T*
(- Aspirin hydrolyzes to salicylic acid and acetate.) Tj
T*
(- Sodium hydroxide acts as a base in the reaction.) Tj
T*
(- Thin-layer chromatography was used to monitor the reaction.) Tj
T*
(- Salicylic acid was detected after 15 minutes.) Tj
